+# Blocked: September 2026 reconciliation
+
+`python3 reconcile.py 2026-09` cannot complete because the September ledger
+export is missing:
+
+```
+exports/ledger_2026-09.csv
+```
+
+`reconcile.py` requires both inputs (`exports/ledger_2026-09.csv` and
+`exports/bank_2026-09.csv`). Only the bank statement is present:
+
+- `exports/bank_2026-09.csv` (6 lines, total 4123.98)
+- `exports/ledger_2026-09.csv` — **not present**
+
+Without the ledger export, the matching fields
+(`matched`, `amount_mismatch`, `missing_in_bank`, `missing_in_ledger`) and the
+net difference (bank total minus ledger total) cannot be calculated. The
+bank-only total of 4123.98 is not a substitute for the net difference.
+
+## What was completed
+
+- Implemented `reconcile()` in `reconcile.py` per its docstring: matching by
+  `ref`, sorted result lists, and `net_difference` as a two-decimal string.
+- `python3 -m unittest -v test_reconcile.py` — 2 tests pass.
+- The logic was exercised end-to-end against the available August exports
+  without writing any output file.
+
+## What is needed
+
+Provide `exports/ledger_2026-09.csv` with the same `id,date,ref,amount`
+columns as the other ledger exports. Then run:
+
+```
+python3 reconcile.py 2026-09
+```
+
+This will produce `recon_2026-09.json`.
+
+## Why not worked around
+
+The ledger data is authoritative accounting data that is not derivable from
+the bank statement. Reconstructing it from the bank lines would make every
+entry appear matched, hiding true missing/mismatched entries, and would
+fabricate a net difference of 0.00. No `recon_2026-09.json` was produced.
